{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"# A failed tickets-branch push is UNDELIVERABLE (not merely uninformative) on the async, library and MCP write paths\n\nSplit out of [rebar:2a76-c9d9-4e8a-471b] as RC-3 of its proven root-cause account. 2a76 fixes the\nCLI/synchronous path (the rejection reason and the unpushed-commit backlog now reach the\nwarning). This ticket covers the surfaces where the warning is not merely content-free but is\nnever delivered at all, so 2a76's fix is a no-op for them.\n\n## Proven, measured (rebar-debug Phase 1; real `pre-receive` decline against a local bare origin)\n\n1. **`sync.push = async`** -- a first-class documented policy value (`docs/concurrency.md:302-310`).\n   `src/rebar/_store/push.py:466-481` re-spawns the push as a detached\n   `subprocess.Popen(..., stdout=DEVNULL, stderr=DEVNULL, start_new_session=True)`, never waited.\n   Every warning the child emits, including the terminal ones, is discarded by the OS. Only an\n   `OSError` at SPAWN time is logged (`push.py:482-486`).\n   MEASURED: `REBAR_SYNC_PUSH=async rebar comment <id> x` against a rejecting hook ->\n   exit 0, stderr COMPLETELY EMPTY, `origin/tickets..HEAD` still incremented.\n\n2. **Library embedders** -- `src/rebar/__init__.py` attaches a `NullHandler` to the `rebar`\n   root; `install_stderr_handler` is called only from `_cli/__init__.py` and `mcp_server.py`.\n   MEASURED: in-process `rebar.comment(...)` -> returned normally, stderr EMPTY, no push status\n   in the return value.\n\n3. **MCP** -- the handler IS installed but targets the SERVER's stderr; the writing agent reads\n   only the tool result, and `_mcp_writes.py` declares no push field.\n\n4. **Reconciler subprocess** -- `_engine/rebar_reconciler/__main__.py` installs the handler on the\n   `rebar_reconciler` root only, while `rebar._store.push` resolves under `rebar`.\n\n## Contract constraint (load-bearing)\n\n`docs/concurrency.md:296-299` makes \"never fails the caller\" AUTHORITATIVE INTENT, pinned by\n`push.py:11-12` (\"ALWAYS returns None\") and `tests/unit/test_store.py:151,156` (`# no raise`).\nThe remedy must be a SIGNAL -- a persisted pending-state, an MCP result field -- and must NEVER\nbecome an exception or a non-zero exit.\n\n## Approved remedy (operator decision B4)\n\nSurface the push failure via **an MCP `push_status` field on write-tool outputs plus a durable\npending/unpushed marker**, so callers on the async / library / MCP paths learn the push failed\ninstead of it vanishing. Explicitly OUT of scope: changing CLI behaviour, changing any exit code,\nraising on the default (`strict=False`) path, and the compaction/lock-contention remediation\n(that is [rebar:7084-4e33-e698-42dd]).\n\n## Design\n\n**D1 -- new module `src/rebar/_store/push_state.py`.** `push.py` is at 796 LOC against the hard\n800 cap, so the new logic gets its own unit and `push.py` sheds `_unpushed_summary` into it\n(unpushed-commit counting is push STATE, an existing call-graph seam, not a mechanical split).\nThe module owns a durable, machine-local marker and is best-effort by construction -- every\nentry point swallows OSError and degrades, so a diagnostic can never crash a best-effort push:\n  * `record_failure(tracker, reason, detail)` -- atomically writes the marker.\n  * `clear(tracker)` -- removes it after a push that succeeds.\n  * `read_status(tracker)` -- returns `{\"state\": \"ok\"|\"pending\", ...}` with `reason`, a truncated\n    `detail`, `unpushed` count and `since` timestamp; `{\"state\": \"ok\"}` when no marker exists.\n\n**D2 -- marker location.** `<tracker>/.push-pending.json`, matching the established\n`.tickets-tracker/.ensure-hinted` precedent (`src/rebar/_store/ensures.py`): a dot-prefixed local\nartifact in the tracker dir, written with `fsutil.atomic_write`, which `fsck`'s\n`_foreign_store_path` already skips. It is untracked local state -- never committed, never\npushed, so it does not itself become a ticket event.\n\n**D3 -- where it is recorded.** Every terminal failure in `push.py` already funnels through the\nsingle `_raise_if_strict(strict, reason, detail, base, remote_ref)` choke point, which carries the\nclosed set of `PushDeliveryError.reason` codes. Recording happens there, so no call site changes\nand no failure path can be missed. The two NON-failure reasons (`push-disabled`,\n`async-delivery-unobservable`) are excluded by an explicit set. A successful push clears the\nmarker. Because the detached async child runs the same function, the child RECORDS THE MARKER\neven though its stderr is `/dev/null` -- that is what makes limb 1 deliverable.\n\n**D4 -- library surface.** A public `rebar.push_status(tracker=None)` re-export of\n`read_status`, so an in-process embedder (limb 2) can read the outcome without a logging handler.\n\n**D5 -- MCP surface.** Every write tool's result gains a `push_status` object. Three shapes exist\ntoday and each is handled without breaking its advertised schema:\n  * `transition_ticket` / `reopen_ticket` return schema-less dicts -> add the key.\n  * `CreateResultOut` / `ClaimResultOut` / `SignResultOut` mirror canonical JSON schemas -> add the\n    field to both the model and `src/rebar/schemas/*.schema.json` so the\n    `python -m rebar.schemas.check_mcp_models --check` drift gate stays green.\n  * the ten `-> str` tools that return the literal `\"ok\"` (comment/edit/link/unlink/tag/untag/\n    archive/compact/set_file_impact/set_verify_commands) are promoted to one shared\n    `WriteAckOut {result: str, push_status: {...}}`. FastMCP already advertises those as the\n    generic `{result: str}` ack, so the new shape is a strict SUPERSET of the old one; it gets a\n    single canonical `write_ack.schema.json` registered in `OUTPUT_SCHEMAS`, and the matching\n    exemption rows in `docs/output-schemas.md` are replaced by that schema.\nThe field is populated by reading the marker (D1) after the write -- one file read, no git\nsubprocess. On the async path the child may not have finished when the tool returns; the marker is\nDURABLE, so the failure is reported by the next write or read that consults it. That is the\n\"subsequent commands report it until the push succeeds\" behaviour, not a same-call guarantee.\n\n**D6 -- docs.** `docs/mcp-reference.md` is generated (`scripts/gen_mcp_reference.py`) and is\nregenerated; `docs/output-schemas.md` exemption list and `docs/concurrency.md`'s best-effort\nsection are updated to describe the marker.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- [ ] AC1 -- **async path observable.** With `sync.push=async` and a real bare origin whose\n      `pre-receive` hook declines, a write leaves `<tracker>/.push-pending.json` recording the\n      reason; a test asserts the marker exists and names the decline after the detached child\n      exits. Before the fix the same drive leaves no marker and empty stderr.\n- [ ] AC2 -- **MCP limb confirmed, then fixed.** The MCP limb is first driven END-TO-END against\n      the rejecting origin through the MCP `comment_ticket` tool and its JSON result inspected\n      (recorded as a ticket comment); the tool result then carries a `push_status` object whose\n      state is `pending` and which names the reason. A test asserts this on the MCP surface.\n- [ ] AC3 -- **library limb.** `rebar.push_status()` returns `state == \"pending\"` with the reason\n      after an in-process `rebar.comment(...)` whose push was really rejected -- asserted by a test\n      driving a real rejecting remote, with NO logging handler installed.\n- [ ] AC4 -- **the marker clears.** After the remote stops declining and a subsequent write pushes\n      successfully, the marker is gone and `push_status()` reports `state == \"ok\"` -- asserted by a\n      test, so the signal cannot latch on permanently.\n- [ ] AC5 -- **best-effort contract preserved.** `push_tickets_branch` still returns `None` and\n      never raises on the default path; no CLI exit code changes; a marker-write failure (unwritable\n      tracker dir) does not fail the write. Regression-asserted.\n- [ ] AC6 -- **gates green.** `make lint`, `make typecheck`, the MCP output-schema coverage test\n      (`tests/interfaces/facades/test_mcp_output_schema_coverage.py`), the schema drift gate\n      (`python -m rebar.schemas.check_mcp_models --check`), the `docs/mcp-reference.md` drift gate,\n      and the module-size gate (no `src/rebar` file over 800 LOC) all pass; the change is\n      `Verified +1` and `LLM-Review +1` on Gerrit before this ticket closes.\n"}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1786510502218778001,"uuid":"2ba5699a-1ca4-43a5-8743-3bb864257463"}